<examples>
The following examples illustrate the expected level of rigour and the tone of a well-formed estimation. They are reference outputs from past projects; do not copy their numbers, use them only as a calibration anchor.

<example>
<project_description>
A data pipeline to consolidate sales data from five regional CRMs into a single warehouse. Nightly batch ingestion, schema normalisation, deduplication of customer records, and a dashboard refreshed every morning for the commercial team. No real-time requirement.
</project_description>
<estimation>
| phase            | duration_weeks | cost_eur | confidence_pct |
|------------------|----------------|----------|----------------|
| Discovery        | 2              | 4,000    | 80             |
| Design           | 2              | 4,500    | 75             |
| Implementation   | 7              | 24,500   | 65             |
| QA               | 2              | 5,000    | 70             |
| Launch           | 1              | 2,000    | 80             |

Totals: 640 total_hours, 40,000 total_cost_eur, 14 total_duration_weeks.
Team: 1 data engineer, 1 backend developer (part-time), 1 analytics engineer for the dashboard.
</estimation>
</example>

<example>
<project_description>
Web SaaS for independent gyms to manage class bookings: member portal, class calendar with waitlists, recurring payments via Stripe, and an admin panel for instructors to track attendance. Single tenant per gym, no native mobile app for this phase.
</project_description>
<estimation>
| phase            | duration_weeks | cost_eur | confidence_pct |
|------------------|----------------|----------|----------------|
| Discovery        | 1              | 2,500    | 85             |
| Design           | 2              | 4,500    | 80             |
| Implementation   | 8              | 27,000   | 65             |
| QA               | 2              | 5,000    | 70             |
| Launch           | 1              | 2,000    | 80             |

Totals: 640 total_hours, 41,000 total_cost_eur, 14 total_duration_weeks.
Team: 1 product designer (part-time), 2 full-stack developers, 1 QA engineer (part-time).
</estimation>
</example>

<example>
